n \\(j\\in[n]\\) let \\(i_j\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i_j)=j\\) (the uncovered square in column \\(j\\)).  Define the rectangle\n\\[\nT_j=\\{\\,(i,j)\\mid i\\in[n]\\setminus\\{i_j\\}\\}.\n\\]\nGeometrically, \\(T_j\\) is a vertical strip of width one and height \\(n-1\\); its sides lie on grid lines and it contains no square of \\(U\\) because the only square of column \\(j\\) that is not in \\(T_j\\) is precisely \\((i_j,j)\\in U\\).\nThe strips \\(T_1,\\dots,T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint (they occupy different columns) and together cover every square of \\(S\\).  Hence\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le n=2025.\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound \u2013 at least \\(n\\) tiles are necessary\nAssume that a tiling of \\(S\\) uses \\(t\\) rectangular tiles.  For each tile \\(R\\) define its **indicator matrix** \\(M_R\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times n}\\) by\n\\[\n(M_R)_{i,j}=\\begin{cases}1,&\\text{if }(i,j)\\in R,\\\\[2pt]0,&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\n\\]\nBecause the tiles are disjoint and together cover \\(S\\), the sum of these matrices equals the matrix that records exactly the squares of \\(S\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{t} M_{R_k}=J-P,\\tag{1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(J\\) denotes the \\(n\\times n\\) all\u2011ones matrix and \\(P\\) denotes the permutation matrix of \\(\\sigma\\) (i.e. \\(P_{i,\\sigma(i)}=1\\) and all other entries are \\(0\\)).  The right\u2011hand side of (1) has a \\(1\\) in every position of \\(S\\) and a \\(0\\) exactly at the uncovered squares.\n\nEach rectangle \\(R\\) is a Cartesian product of a set of rows \\(A\\subseteq[n]\\) and a set of columns \\(B\\subseteq[n]\\) with \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) intervals of consecutive indices.  Its indicator matrix can be written as an outer product\n\\[\nM_R=\\mathbf{u}\\mathbf{v}^\\top,\\qquad\\mathbf{u}_i=\\begin{cases}1,&i\\in A,\\\\0,&i\\notin A,\\end{cases}\\quad\\mathbf{v}_j=\\begin{cases}1,&j\\in B,\\\\0,&j\\notin B.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThus \\(M_R\\) has rank at most \\(1\\) (rank \\(0\\) only if the rectangle is empty, which never occurs in a tiling).  Consequently the sum of the \\(t\\) indicator matrices has rank at most \\(t\\):\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}\\Bigl(\\sum_{k=1}^{t} M_{R_k}\\Bigr)\\le t.\n\\]\nFrom (1) we obtain\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)\\le t.\n\\]\nIt remains to compute \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)\\).  The permutation matrix \\(P\\) is invertible, hence has rank \\(n\\).  The all\u2011ones matrix \\(J\\) has rank \\(1\\).  Since \\(P\\) is invertible, we can write\n\\[\nJ-P = P\\bigl(P^{-1}J-I\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe matrix \\(P^{-1}J\\) has every row equal to the all\u2011ones row vector, so it has rank \\(1\\).  Subtracting the identity matrix \\(I\\) (rank \\(n\\)) from a rank\u20111 matrix yields a matrix of rank \\(n\\); indeed, for any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) the eigenvalues of \\(J-P\\) are \\(n-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(1\\)) and \\(-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)), so the matrix is nonsingular and its rank is \\(n\\).  Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n.\n\\]\nCombining this with the previous inequality gives \\(t\\ge n\\).\nThus any tiling of \\(S\\) requires at least \\(n\\) rectangular tiles.\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion\nWe have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles (the vertical strips) and proved that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** The minimum number of tiles Matilda must place is 2025.",
